+{"question_index": 3, "question_id": 2816, "category": "biology", "k": 0, "gold": "G", "parsed_letter": "I", "correct": false, "full_text": "*   Tay-Sachs is a recessive disease.\n    *   Individuals with the disease (homozygous recessive) usually die before reproducing.\n    *   The question asks how the allele persists in the population despite the disease being lethal.\n\n    *   A. Occasionally the gene will instead increase the fitness of the individual. (Not typical for Tay-Sachs).\n    *   B. Tay-Sachs is asymptomatic in people who are homozygous recessive. (Incorrect, it's a lethal disease).\n    *   C. The recessive allele is actually beneficial in certain environments. (Similar to A, not standard for Tay-Sachs).\n    *   D. The disease increases the fertility of those who carry it. (Unlikely).\n    *   E. Tay-Sachs is a dominant disease... (Incorrect, it's recessive).\n    *   F. Spontaneous mutation converts the dominant allele... (Mutations occur, but this doesn't explain persistence).\n    *   G. Heterozygous individuals will show no symptoms. (This means carriers have the allele but aren't affected).\n    *   H. The gene for Tay-Sachs also provides resistance to another disease. (Possible, but less common explanation than the carrier status).\n    *   I. The disease can be cured... (Incorrect, Tay-Sachs is generally incurable).\n    *   J. The allele is not preserved... (The question asks how it *is* preserved).\n\n    *   Tay-Sachs is a classic example of a recessive genetic disorder where the *carriers* (heterozygotes) are unaffected.\n    *   Because carriers do not show symptoms, they can reproduce and pass the recessive allele to their offspring.\n    *   If the disease were dominant, anyone with the allele would show symptoms and die, causing the allele to be eliminated from the gene pool (unless it provided\n"}
+{"question_index": 3, "question_id": 2816, "category": "biology", "k": 1, "gold": "G", "parsed_letter": "J", "correct": false, "full_text": "*   Disease: Tay-Sachs (autosomal recessive).\n    *   Problem: Individuals with the disease (homozygous recessive) usually die young and don't reproduce.\n    *   Question: How is the allele (and the disease) preserved in the population?\n\n    *   A. Occasionally the gene will instead increase the fitness of the individual. (Possible, but less likely to be the primary explanation for Tay-Sachs specifically).\n    *   B. Tay-Sachs is asymptomatic in people who are homozygous recessive. (False, the question says they have the disease and die young).\n    *   C. The recessive allele is actually beneficial in certain environments. (Possible, but \"heterozygote advantage\" is a more common explanation for recessive alleles).\n    *   D. The disease increases the fertility of those who carry it. (Unlikely).\n    *   E. Tay-Sachs is a dominant disease, so carriers will show symptoms. (False, it's recessive).\n    *   F. Spontaneous mutation converts the dominant allele to the recessive form. (Mutation happens, but doesn't explain persistence if the recessive phenotype is lethal).\n    *   G. Heterozygous individuals will show no symptoms. (This is the definition of a recessive trait. If the allele is carried by heterozygotes who *do* survive and reproduce, the allele can be passed on).\n    *   H. The gene for Tay-Sachs also provides resistance to another disease. (This is a specific type of heterozygote advantage, similar to sickle cell anemia and malaria).\n    *   I. The disease can be cured, so individuals with the disorder are able to reproduce. (False, Tay-Sachs is currently incurable).\n    *   J. The allele is not preserved and is actually decreasing in frequency. (Possible, but the question asks how it *is* preserved).\n"}
