+{"question_index": 5, "question_id": 10398, "category": "computer science", "k": 5, "gold": "C", "parsed_letter": "C", "correct": true, "full_text": "*   The question asks: \"When the data must arrive at the receiver exactly as they were sent, its called...\"\n    *   Context: Information Security / Network Security.\n\n    *   A. Message Security: A broad term for all security measures.\n    *   B. Message Clarity: Not a standard security term in this context.\n    *   C. Message Integrity: Ensuring the data has not been altered or tampered with during transmission (i.e., it arrives exactly as it was sent).\n    *   D. Message Authenticity: Verifying that the message comes from the claimed sender.\n    *   E. Message Splashing: Not a standard term.\n    *   F. Message Reliability: Relates to the consistency of the data delivery, but \"Integrity\" is the specific security term for \"exactly as sent.\"\n    *   G. Message Sending: The act of sending a message.\n    *   H. Message Stability: Not a standard security term.\n    *   I. Message Confidentiality: Ensuring the data is only readable by the intended recipient.\n    *   J. Message Completion: Ensuring the whole message is received, but \"Integrity\" is the more common term for \"exactly as sent\" (no changes).\n\n    *   *Integrity* is the standard term in information security (CIA triad: Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability) that specifically refers to ensuring that information is accurate, complete, and has not been modified by an unauthorized party or during transmission.\n\n    *   A: Message Security (too broad)\n    *   C: Message Integrity (matches \"exactly as they were sent\")\n    *   D: Message Authenticity (different concept: who sent it?)\n    *   I: Message Confidentiality (different concept: who can read it?)\n\n    *   \"When the data must arrive at the receiver exactly as they were\n"}
+{"question_index": 6, "question_id": 6624, "category": "health", "k": 0, "gold": "C", "parsed_letter": "C", "correct": true, "full_text": "*   A. Controlling food prices. (Incorrect, price is a factor, but not the definition).\n    *   B. Preventing terrorists from poisoning food. (Incorrect, this is food safety).\n    *   C. Component elements include availability, utilization, stability, and access. (This is a standard definition of food security).\n    *   D. Development of GM crops. (Incorrect, this is a method to *achieve* food security, not the definition).\n    *   E. Protecting food from natural disasters. (Incorrect, this is a specific aspect of stability).\n    *   F. Ensuring everyone's access to food. (This is a part of it, but less complete than C).\n    *   G. Ending micronutrient malnutrition. (Incorrect, this is a subset of food security/nutrition).\n    *   H. System of producing enough food. (Incorrect, this is food production/availability).\n    *   I. Protection of agricultural land. (Incorrect).\n\n    *   The standard definition of food security (e.g., by the FAO) includes four main pillars:\n        1.  Availability (Is there enough food?)\n        2.  Access (Can people get the food?)\n        3.  Utilization (Is the food nutritious and safe?)\n        4.  Stability (Is the food available and accessible consistently?)\n\n    *   Option C: \"Its component elements include availability, utilisation, and stability, as well as access.\"\n    *   This matches the standard pillars exactly.\n\n    *   Option F: \"It is about ensuring everyone's access to food.\" (While true, it's only one pillar).\n    *   Option H: \"It is the system of producing enough food to feed a country's population.\" (Only availability).\n\n    *   The question asks \"What is food security?\" and option C\n"}
+{"question_index": 6, "question_id": 6624, "category": "health", "k": 1, "gold": "C", "parsed_letter": "C", "correct": true, "full_text": "The question asks for the definition of \"food security.\"\n\n    *   A. Ensuring food prices are controlled and affordable (part of access, but not the whole definition).\n    *   B. Preventing terrorists from poisoning food (not a definition).\n    *   C. Component elements include availability, utilisation, and stability, as well as access. (This is a classic definition from the FAO).\n    *   D. Development of genetically modified crops (a method, not a definition).\n    *   E. Protecting food supplies from natural disasters (a part of stability, not the definition).\n    *   F. Ensuring everyone's access to food (part of the definition, but incomplete).\n    *   G. Ending micronutrient malnutrition (a goal, not the definition).\n    *   H. System of producing enough food (part of the definition, but incomplete).\n    *   I. Protection of agricultural land (not the definition).\n\n    *   The Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) of the United Nations defines food security as: \"Food security exists when all people, at all times, have physical, social, and economic access to sufficient, safe, and nutritious food which meets their dietary needs and food preferences for an active and healthy life.\"\n    *   The four pillars of food security are:\n        1.  Availability (Is there enough food?)\n        2.  Access (Can people get it?)\n        3.  Utilization (Is it nutritious and safe to eat?)\n        4.  Stability (Is the supply constant?)\n\n    *   Option C states: \"Its component elements include availability, utilisation, and stability, as well as access.\" This matches the four pillars of the standard definition of food security.\n\n    *   A: Only \"access\" (price/affordability).\n    *   B: Not related to the definition.\n    *   C\n"}
+{"question_index": 6, "question_id": 6624, "category": "health", "k": 2, "gold": "C", "parsed_letter": "D", "correct": false, "full_text": "*   A. Food prices controlled and affordable. (Part of food security, but not the definition.)\n    *   B. Preventing terrorists from poisoning food. (Not the definition.)\n    *   C. Components include availability, utilization, stability, and access. (This is the standard FAO/UN definition.)\n    *   D. Development of genetically modified crops. (A method to achieve food security, not the definition.)\n    *   E. Protecting food supplies from natural disasters. (A part of stability, but not the definition.)\n    *   F. Ensuring everyone's access to food. (Simplified definition, but C is more comprehensive and technically accurate.)\n    *   G. Ending micronutrient malnutrition. (Part of utilization, but not the whole definition.)\n    *   H. Producing enough food to feed a population. (Related to availability, but not the whole definition.)\n    *   I. Protection of agricultural land. (Related to sustainability, not the definition.)\n\n    *   The Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) of the United Nations defines food security as \"having access to sufficient, safe and nutritious food that meets all dietary needs and food preferences for an active and healthy life.\"\n    *   The four pillars of food security are:\n        1.  Availability (Is there enough food?)\n        2.  Access (Can people get the food?)\n        3.  Utilization (Is the food nutritious and safe?)\n        4.  Stability (Is the supply consistent over time?)\n\n    *   Option C: \"Its component elements include availability, utilisation, and stability, as well as access.\"\n    *   This aligns perfectly with the standard definition of the four pillars of food security.\n\n    *   A: Incorrect (narrow)\n    *   B: Incorrect (irrelevant)\n    *   C: Correct (standard definition)\n    *   D\n"}
+{"question_index": 6, "question_id": 6624, "category": "health", "k": 3, "gold": "C", "parsed_letter": "C", "correct": true, "full_text": "*   A: Food price control (part of access, but not the full definition).\n    *   B: Preventing terrorism (not the definition).\n    *   C: Availability, utilization, stability, and access (this is the standard FAO definition).\n    *   D: Genetically modified crops (a method to achieve food security, not the definition).\n    *   E: Protection from disasters (part of stability, but not the full definition).\n    *   F: Ensuring everyone's access to food (a part of the definition, but less complete than C).\n    *   G: Ending micronutrient malnutrition (part of utilization, but not the full definition).\n    *   H: System of producing enough food (part of availability, but not the full definition).\n    *   I: Protection of agricultural land (not the definition).\n\n    The Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) defines food security as: \"Food security exists when all people, at all times, have physical, social, and economic access to sufficient, safe, and nutritious food which meets their dietary needs and food preferences for an active and healthy life.\"\n\n    This definition is broken down into four pillars:\n    1. Availability (Is there enough food?)\n    2. Access (Can people get it?)\n    3. Utilization (Is the food nutritious and safe?)\n    4. Stability (Is the supply consistent over time?)\n\n    Option C lists: \"availability, utilisation, and stability, as well as access.\" This matches the four pillars perfectly.\n\n    *   F: \"ensuring everyone's access to food\" - This is only one pillar (Access).\n    *   H: \"system of producing enough food\" - This is only one pillar (Availability).\n    *   C: \"availability, utilisation, and stability, as well as access\" - This covers all pillars.\n\n    Wait\n"}
+{"question_index": 6, "question_id": 6624, "category": "health", "k": 4, "gold": "C", "parsed_letter": "I", "correct": false, "full_text": "*   A. Food prices control/affordability (Part of access, but not the whole definition).\n    *   B. Preventing terrorist poisoning (Irrelevant to the definition of food security).\n    *   C. Availability, utilization, stability, and access (The four pillars of food security).\n    *   D. Genetically modified crops (A method to *achieve* food security, not the definition).\n    *   E. Protecting from natural disasters (A way to maintain food security, not the definition).\n    *   F. Ensuring everyone's access to food (A simplified definition, but not the comprehensive one).\n    *   G. Ending micronutrient malnutrition (A component of food security, specifically utilization).\n    *   H. Producing enough food (Availability, but not the whole definition).\n    *   I. Protection of agricultural land (Related to food security, but not the definition).\n\n    *   Food security is generally defined by the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) of the United Nations as \"having access to enough safe and nutritious food... [that] meets their dietary needs and food preferences for an active and healthy life.\"\n    *   The four pillars are:\n        1.  Availability (Is there enough food?)\n        2.  Access (Can people get it?)\n        3.  Utilization (Is the food nutritious and safe?)\n        4.  Stability (Is the food available consistently over time?)\n\n    *   Option C: \"Its component elements include availability, utilisation, and stability, as well as access.\" matches the standard definition perfectly.\n\n    *   A. (Prices) - Only part of access.\n    *   B. (Terrorists) - Incorrect.\n    *   C. (Availability, utilization, stability, access) - Correct.\n    *   D. (GMOs) - Incorrect.\n    *\n"}
